COPY public.case_files (id, cluster_id, summary, confidence_note, suggested_action, key_signals, caveats, raw_response, model, prompt_version, cluster_score_at_generation, detector_version, generated_at, cost_usd, input_tokens, output_tokens, duration_ms, usage_json) FROM stdin;
17673796-8166-4056-8f14-c18f30af6859	2f2df187-bb34-4681-bc3f-dc96b1975744	Four accounts made 21 purchases on a single day (28 Aug), all paying with the same card, and three of them ship to the same address. The shared card covers 12 of those transactions and the shared address 8. Amounts are ordinary retail sizes (3,328–8,608 INR, median 6,128). The timing looks like people clicking, not scripts: gaps average around 7.5 minutes and are irregular. One of the four accounts has three or fewer transactions, which can mean a throwaway account made for a signup discount, but three of the four look established. The obvious innocent read is a household or small group sharing one card and one delivery address during a sale day. Nothing here separates that from a discount-abuse ring yet.	Moderate but unresolved — the sharing is real and concentrated in a single day, yet a shared card plus shared address is exactly what a family looks like, and the human-paced timing argues against automation.	review_closer	["one card used by all 4 accounts across 12 transactions", "one address shared by 3 accounts across 8 transactions", "all 21 transactions on a single day", "one account with 3 or fewer transactions ever", "manual, irregular pacing (about 7.5 minutes between purchases)"]	["A household or shared flat using one card and one delivery address explains everything shown here.", "Check whether the accounts' names, phones, and emails look like distinct real people or like variations on one identity.", "Check whether first-order or referral discounts were applied — repeated new-customer discounts on one card would push this toward coordinated abuse.", "Check account ages and prior history: four accounts all created shortly before 28 Aug would be much harder to explain innocently.", "The evidence contains no device-sharing data and no history before 28 Aug, so this may be one day out of a longer normal pattern."]	{"caveats": ["A household or shared flat using one card and one delivery address explains everything shown here.", "Check whether the accounts' names, phones, and emails look like distinct real people or like variations on one identity.", "Check whether first-order or referral discounts were applied — repeated new-customer discounts on one card would push this toward coordinated abuse.", "Check account ages and prior history: four accounts all created shortly before 28 Aug would be much harder to explain innocently.", "The evidence contains no device-sharing data and no history before 28 Aug, so this may be one day out of a longer normal pattern."], "summary": "Four accounts made 21 purchases on a single day (28 Aug), all paying with the same card, and three of them ship to the same address. The shared card covers 12 of those transactions and the shared address 8. Amounts are ordinary retail sizes (3,328–8,608 INR, median 6,128). The timing looks like people clicking, not scripts: gaps average around 7.5 minutes and are irregular. One of the four accounts has three or fewer transactions, which can mean a throwaway account made for a signup discount, but three of the four look established. The obvious innocent read is a household or small group sharing one card and one delivery address during a sale day. Nothing here separates that from a discount-abuse ring yet.", "confidence": "Moderate but unresolved — the sharing is real and concentrated in a single day, yet a shared card plus shared address is exactly what a family looks like, and the human-paced timing argues against automation.", "key_signals": ["one card used by all 4 accounts across 12 transactions", "one address shared by 3 accounts across 8 transactions", "all 21 transactions on a single day", "one account with 3 or fewer transactions ever", "manual, irregular pacing (about 7.5 minutes between purchases)"], "suggested_action": "review_closer"}	claude-opus-5	case-file-v1	0.42774	0.5.0	2026-08-30 04:04:31.298562+00	0.143891	12688	617	10553	{"usage": {"speed": "standard", "iterations": [{"type": "message", "input_tokens": 2, "output_tokens": 617, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 12686, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 12686}], "input_tokens": 2, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available", "output_tokens": 617, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 12686, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "server_tool_use": {"web_fetch_requests": 0, "web_search_requests": 0}, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 0}, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 12686}, "model_usage": {"claude-opus-5": {"costUSD": 0.142295, "provider": "firstParty", "inputTokens": 2, "outputTokens": 617, "contextWindow": 1000000, "canonicalModel": "claude-opus-5", "maxOutputTokens": 64000, "webSearchRequests": 0, "cacheReadInputTokens": 0, "cacheCreationInputTokens": 12686}, "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001": {"costUSD": 0.001596, "provider": "firstParty", "inputTokens": 1491, "outputTokens": 21, "contextWindow": 200000, "canonicalModel": "claude-haiku-4-5", "maxOutputTokens": 32000, "webSearchRequests": 0, "cacheReadInputTokens": 0, "cacheCreationInputTokens": 0}}}
79ba4867-78cb-4239-b1c9-a161135b1d57	de3688ed-e5fd-4dd6-a54b-104551f17175	Four accounts all used the same address on 6 of their 9 transactions, all on 28 August. Every one of the four is brand new, with three or fewer transactions each — that is the strongest signal here, and it's the pattern you'd see if someone spun up throwaway accounts to reuse a first-order discount. But the shared address is the only shared attribute: no shared device, no shared payment card in this evidence. The pace is unhurried (about 84 seconds between transactions) and irregular in a human way, not automated. Values are ordinary retail amounts, 482–1560 INR, 9482 INR total. A household, shared flat, hostel, or office where four people ordered the same day fits this evidence about as well as coordination does — new accounts and one day of activity are normal for a household that just discovered the platform.	Low to moderate confidence either way — the account newness is real but the shared address alone is weak, and the detector's own score of 0.386 reflects that. I'd want device or card overlap before leaning toward a ring.	review_closer	["all 4 accounts brand new, 3 or fewer transactions each", "one shared address across 6 of 9 transactions", "all activity compressed into a single day (28 Aug)", "timing looks manual, not scripted — ~84s median gap, irregular", "ordinary retail amounts, nothing structured or round"]	["A shared address is a household, shared flat, hostel, or workplace far more often than a fraud ring — this is the strongest innocent explanation", "Would lean toward a ring if the accounts also share a device fingerprint, payment card, or phone number — none of which appear in this evidence", "Would lean toward a ring if all four accounts redeemed the same first-order promotion; the evidence doesn't say whether any discount was applied", "Would lean toward a household if names on the accounts differ but plausibly co-reside, or if the address is a known multi-unit or commercial building", "Only one day of activity is visible; if these accounts go dormant after the first order that supports the sock-puppet read, if they keep ordering normally it does not"]	{"caveats": ["A shared address is a household, shared flat, hostel, or workplace far more often than a fraud ring — this is the strongest innocent explanation", "Would lean toward a ring if the accounts also share a device fingerprint, payment card, or phone number — none of which appear in this evidence", "Would lean toward a ring if all four accounts redeemed the same first-order promotion; the evidence doesn't say whether any discount was applied", "Would lean toward a household if names on the accounts differ but plausibly co-reside, or if the address is a known multi-unit or commercial building", "Only one day of activity is visible; if these accounts go dormant after the first order that supports the sock-puppet read, if they keep ordering normally it does not"], "summary": "Four accounts all used the same address on 6 of their 9 transactions, all on 28 August. Every one of the four is brand new, with three or fewer transactions each — that is the strongest signal here, and it's the pattern you'd see if someone spun up throwaway accounts to reuse a first-order discount. But the shared address is the only shared attribute: no shared device, no shared payment card in this evidence. The pace is unhurried (about 84 seconds between transactions) and irregular in a human way, not automated. Values are ordinary retail amounts, 482–1560 INR, 9482 INR total. A household, shared flat, hostel, or office where four people ordered the same day fits this evidence about as well as coordination does — new accounts and one day of activity are normal for a household that just discovered the platform.", "confidence": "Low to moderate confidence either way — the account newness is real but the shared address alone is weak, and the detector's own score of 0.386 reflects that. I'd want device or card overlap before leaning toward a ring.", "key_signals": ["all 4 accounts brand new, 3 or fewer transactions each", "one shared address across 6 of 9 transactions", "all activity compressed into a single day (28 Aug)", "timing looks manual, not scripted — ~84s median gap, irregular", "ordinary retail amounts, nothing structured or round"], "suggested_action": "review_closer"}	claude-opus-5	case-file-v1	0.386468	0.5.0	2026-08-30 04:04:33.204032+00	0.146572	12674	731	12369	{"usage": {"speed": "standard", "iterations": [{"type": "message", "input_tokens": 2, "output_tokens": 731, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 12672, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 12672}], "input_tokens": 2, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available", "output_tokens": 731, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 12672, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "server_tool_use": {"web_fetch_requests": 0, "web_search_requests": 0}, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 0}, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 12672}, "model_usage": {"claude-opus-5": {"costUSD": 0.145005, "provider": "firstParty", "inputTokens": 2, "outputTokens": 731, "contextWindow": 1000000, "canonicalModel": "claude-opus-5", "maxOutputTokens": 64000, "webSearchRequests": 0, "cacheReadInputTokens": 0, "cacheCreationInputTokens": 12672}, "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001": {"costUSD": 0.001567, "provider": "firstParty", "inputTokens": 1477, "outputTokens": 18, "contextWindow": 200000, "canonicalModel": "claude-haiku-4-5", "maxOutputTokens": 32000, "webSearchRequests": 0, "cacheReadInputTokens": 0, "cacheCreationInputTokens": 0}}}
5370d4da-099f-4216-b279-9118e6bb383a	21326362-89ea-4cfb-ba78-4324450dcb89	Eight accounts placed 25 orders on a single day, 28 August, worth about 19,000 INR in total. They all share one delivery address, seven of them share one device, and six share a second device. The timing is the strongest signal: transactions came about 1.6 seconds apart, almost perfectly evenly spaced, against a typical gap of around a day and a half for normal accounts here. No person clicks that fast that regularly, so this looks scripted rather than typed. Six of the eight accounts have three transactions or fewer, and the order values (499-1199 INR) are the size you would expect for signup-discount abuse. A shared address alone would usually mean a household or a shared building, but a household does not check out eight accounts 1.6 seconds apart.	Fairly confident this is coordinated automation rather than ordinary customers, because the sub-two-second, near-metronomic timing is very hard to produce by hand. Less certain about intent - whether it is a fraud ring, a reseller, or one person farming first-order discounts.	likely_ring	["25 transactions, all on a single day", "median 1.6 seconds between transactions, almost perfectly evenly spaced", "all 8 accounts on one address; 7 on one device, 6 on a second device", "6 of 8 accounts have 3 or fewer transactions ever", "order values 499-1199 INR, consistent with first-order discount abuse"]	["A shared address by itself is usually a household, a hostel, or an office - it is the timing, not the address, that carries this case.", "If a legitimate merchant, bulk-order tool, or platform integration is involved, scripted timing could be expected; checking whether these accounts belong to a known business or API partner would settle it.", "I have not seen the actual account names, payment instruments, or items ordered. Identical payment cards or names across accounts would strengthen the case; genuinely distinct payers, funding sources, and delivery recipients would weaken it.", "Automated timing could also come from a shared browser autofill or a checkout retry bug; the per-transaction logs would show which."]	{"caveats": ["A shared address by itself is usually a household, a hostel, or an office - it is the timing, not the address, that carries this case.", "If a legitimate merchant, bulk-order tool, or platform integration is involved, scripted timing could be expected; checking whether these accounts belong to a known business or API partner would settle it.", "I have not seen the actual account names, payment instruments, or items ordered. Identical payment cards or names across accounts would strengthen the case; genuinely distinct payers, funding sources, and delivery recipients would weaken it.", "Automated timing could also come from a shared browser autofill or a checkout retry bug; the per-transaction logs would show which."], "summary": "Eight accounts placed 25 orders on a single day, 28 August, worth about 19,000 INR in total. They all share one delivery address, seven of them share one device, and six share a second device. The timing is the strongest signal: transactions came about 1.6 seconds apart, almost perfectly evenly spaced, against a typical gap of around a day and a half for normal accounts here. No person clicks that fast that regularly, so this looks scripted rather than typed. Six of the eight accounts have three transactions or fewer, and the order values (499-1199 INR) are the size you would expect for signup-discount abuse. A shared address alone would usually mean a household or a shared building, but a household does not check out eight accounts 1.6 seconds apart.", "confidence": "Fairly confident this is coordinated automation rather than ordinary customers, because the sub-two-second, near-metronomic timing is very hard to produce by hand. Less certain about intent - whether it is a fraud ring, a reseller, or one person farming first-order discounts.", "key_signals": ["25 transactions, all on a single day", "median 1.6 seconds between transactions, almost perfectly evenly spaced", "all 8 accounts on one address; 7 on one device, 6 on a second device", "6 of 8 accounts have 3 or fewer transactions ever", "order values 499-1199 INR, consistent with first-order discount abuse"], "suggested_action": "likely_ring"}	claude-opus-5	case-file-v1	0.845979	0.5.0	2026-08-30 04:04:33.403235+00	0.147101	12784	705	12516	{"usage": {"speed": "standard", "iterations": [{"type": "message", "input_tokens": 2, "output_tokens": 705, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 12782, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 12782}], "input_tokens": 2, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available", "output_tokens": 705, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 12782, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "server_tool_use": {"web_fetch_requests": 0, "web_search_requests": 0}, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 0}, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 12782}, "model_usage": {"claude-opus-5": {"costUSD": 0.145455, "provider": "firstParty", "inputTokens": 2, "outputTokens": 705, "contextWindow": 1000000, "canonicalModel": "claude-opus-5", "maxOutputTokens": 64000, "webSearchRequests": 0, "cacheReadInputTokens": 0, "cacheCreationInputTokens": 12782}, "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001": {"costUSD": 0.0016460000000000001, "provider": "firstParty", "inputTokens": 1561, "outputTokens": 17, "contextWindow": 200000, "canonicalModel": "claude-haiku-4-5", "maxOutputTokens": 32000, "webSearchRequests": 0, "cacheReadInputTokens": 0, "cacheCreationInputTokens": 0}}}
