ee5efa62-9967-4119-893a-2df14985e997	c10bfc64-2e6c-492f-8a36-0d693307d695	Five accounts are registered to the same address, and four of them used the same device. All 11 transactions happened on one day (2026-08-28), roughly 74 seconds apart, and each of the five accounts has three transactions or fewer. Amounts are small: 451 to 1617 INR, 10,000 INR in total. Five brand-new accounts ordering back-to-back from one device in a single sitting is not how five independent customers behave; it fits someone farming a first-order sign-up discount. The obvious innocent story is a household — one family, one address, one shared phone — but a real household rarely opens five accounts and uses them minutes apart. Note the detector labelled the timing 'human-like', which is misleading here: a 74-second gap is far faster than this platform's typical ~28-hour gap between transactions.	Moderately confident — the stacking of one address, one device, five empty account histories and a single-day burst is hard to explain as coincidence, but I'd stop short of certainty because no shared payment card appears in the evidence and the likely harm is discount abuse rather than stolen funds.	likely_ring	["all 5 accounts at one address", "4 of 5 accounts on one device", "every account has 3 or fewer transactions", "all 11 transactions in one day, ~74 seconds apart", "small, similar order values (451-1617 INR)"]	["A household sharing an address and a phone is the strongest innocent explanation; the timing is what argues against it.", "The evidence says nothing about payment instruments — if all five accounts used the same card, that would firm this up considerably; five genuinely different cards would weaken it.", "The evidence does not say whether a first-order discount or promo code was actually redeemed. If none was, my main theory for the motive is wrong.", "The fifth account is not on the shared device; worth checking whether it is on a separate device at the same address.", "If the accounts belong to five differently-named people who keep transacting independently over the coming weeks, this looks much more like a family than a ring."]	{"caveats": ["A household sharing an address and a phone is the strongest innocent explanation; the timing is what argues against it.", "The evidence says nothing about payment instruments — if all five accounts used the same card, that would firm this up considerably; five genuinely different cards would weaken it.", "The evidence does not say whether a first-order discount or promo code was actually redeemed. If none was, my main theory for the motive is wrong.", "The fifth account is not on the shared device; worth checking whether it is on a separate device at the same address.", "If the accounts belong to five differently-named people who keep transacting independently over the coming weeks, this looks much more like a family than a ring."], "summary": "Five accounts are registered to the same address, and four of them used the same device. All 11 transactions happened on one day (2026-08-28), roughly 74 seconds apart, and each of the five accounts has three transactions or fewer. Amounts are small: 451 to 1617 INR, 10,000 INR in total. Five brand-new accounts ordering back-to-back from one device in a single sitting is not how five independent customers behave; it fits someone farming a first-order sign-up discount. The obvious innocent story is a household — one family, one address, one shared phone — but a real household rarely opens five accounts and uses them minutes apart. Note the detector labelled the timing 'human-like', which is misleading here: a 74-second gap is far faster than this platform's typical ~28-hour gap between transactions.", "confidence": "Moderately confident — the stacking of one address, one device, five empty account histories and a single-day burst is hard to explain as coincidence, but I'd stop short of certainty because no shared payment card appears in the evidence and the likely harm is discount abuse rather than stolen funds.", "key_signals": ["all 5 accounts at one address", "4 of 5 accounts on one device", "every account has 3 or fewer transactions", "all 11 transactions in one day, ~74 seconds apart", "small, similar order values (451-1617 INR)"], "suggested_action": "likely_ring"}	claude-opus-5	case-file-v1	0.673268	0.5.0	2026-08-30 04:14:23.814358+00	0.112054	12711	2619	33139	{"usage": {"speed": "standard", "iterations": [{"type": "message", "input_tokens": 2, "output_tokens": 2619, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 4065, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_read_input_tokens": 8644, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 4065}], "input_tokens": 2, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available", "output_tokens": 2619, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 4065, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "server_tool_use": {"web_fetch_requests": 0, "web_search_requests": 0}, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 1900}, "cache_read_input_tokens": 8644, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 4065}, "model_usage": {"claude-opus-5": {"costUSD": 0.110457, "provider": "firstParty", "inputTokens": 2, "outputTokens": 2619, "contextWindow": 1000000, "canonicalModel": "claude-opus-5", "maxOutputTokens": 64000, "webSearchRequests": 0, "cacheReadInputTokens": 8644, "cacheCreationInputTokens": 4065}, "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001": {"costUSD": 0.0015970000000000001, "provider": "firstParty", "inputTokens": 1507, "outputTokens": 18, "contextWindow": 200000, "canonicalModel": "claude-haiku-4-5", "maxOutputTokens": 32000, "webSearchRequests": 0, "cacheReadInputTokens": 0, "cacheCreationInputTokens": 0}}}
da0d4aa5-4d44-41dc-9878-1bca08608a52	b11fb826-7fbf-4470-b64a-aa475733a1e2	All 55 transactions in this evidence fall on one day, 28 August 2026, and run through just two devices and two payment methods. All six accounts used both devices and both payment methods — complete overlap, not partial. The amounts are tiny: 1 to 50 INR each, 1,220 INR in total. Transactions arrive a few minutes apart, which reads as people tapping through by hand rather than a script. None of the six looks like a throwaway; all have real transaction history. That profile is also exactly what a household, a small shop or agent topping up for walk-in customers, or a shared office phone looks like. The parts that still need explaining are the one-day burst and the very small amounts, which can also be someone testing payment methods with cheap charges before trying larger ones.	Moderate at best. The sharing itself is solid fact, but sharing alone does not separate a shared household or till from a coordinated group, and I cannot see account names, KYC details, who actually owns the two payment methods, or whether the one-day window is real or just the slice of evidence I was given.	review_closer	["all 6 accounts used both shared devices and both shared payment methods", "55 transactions compressed into a single day", "very small amounts: 1-50 INR, 1,220 INR total", "gaps of ~5 minutes, consistent with manual use, not a script", "no throwaway accounts - all six have real history"]	["A family, small shop, or agent sharing two phones and two cards fits this evidence just as well as coordination does.", "Check whether the shared payment methods are registered to one of the six account holders - if so, this is likely benign.", "Check whether these accounts existed and transacted before 28 August, or were all created around it; a longer shared history points toward a household.", "Check for chargebacks or issuer declines on the two payment methods - small test charges on stolen cards would show up there and would flip this toward a ring.", "The total value at stake is about 1,220 INR, so the financial exposure here is minimal regardless of the outcome.", "The evidence only covers one day; I do not know whether that is the account's full history or just the window I was given."]	{"caveats": ["A family, small shop, or agent sharing two phones and two cards fits this evidence just as well as coordination does.", "Check whether the shared payment methods are registered to one of the six account holders - if so, this is likely benign.", "Check whether these accounts existed and transacted before 28 August, or were all created around it; a longer shared history points toward a household.", "Check for chargebacks or issuer declines on the two payment methods - small test charges on stolen cards would show up there and would flip this toward a ring.", "The total value at stake is about 1,220 INR, so the financial exposure here is minimal regardless of the outcome.", "The evidence only covers one day; I do not know whether that is the account's full history or just the window I was given."], "summary": "All 55 transactions in this evidence fall on one day, 28 August 2026, and run through just two devices and two payment methods. All six accounts used both devices and both payment methods — complete overlap, not partial. The amounts are tiny: 1 to 50 INR each, 1,220 INR in total. Transactions arrive a few minutes apart, which reads as people tapping through by hand rather than a script. None of the six looks like a throwaway; all have real transaction history. That profile is also exactly what a household, a small shop or agent topping up for walk-in customers, or a shared office phone looks like. The parts that still need explaining are the one-day burst and the very small amounts, which can also be someone testing payment methods with cheap charges before trying larger ones.", "confidence": "Moderate at best. The sharing itself is solid fact, but sharing alone does not separate a shared household or till from a coordinated group, and I cannot see account names, KYC details, who actually owns the two payment methods, or whether the one-day window is real or just the slice of evidence I was given.", "key_signals": ["all 6 accounts used both shared devices and both shared payment methods", "55 transactions compressed into a single day", "very small amounts: 1-50 INR, 1,220 INR total", "gaps of ~5 minutes, consistent with manual use, not a script", "no throwaway accounts - all six have real history"], "suggested_action": "review_closer"}	claude-opus-5	case-file-v1	0.576906	0.5.0	2026-08-30 04:15:00.313723+00	0.11271	12760	2624	31893	{"usage": {"speed": "standard", "iterations": [{"type": "message", "input_tokens": 2, "output_tokens": 2624, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 4114, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_read_input_tokens": 8644, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 4114}], "input_tokens": 2, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available", "output_tokens": 2624, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 4114, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "server_tool_use": {"web_fetch_requests": 0, "web_search_requests": 0}, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 1872}, "cache_read_input_tokens": 8644, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 4114}, "model_usage": {"claude-opus-5": {"costUSD": 0.111072, "provider": "firstParty", "inputTokens": 2, "outputTokens": 2624, "contextWindow": 1000000, "canonicalModel": "claude-opus-5", "maxOutputTokens": 64000, "webSearchRequests": 0, "cacheReadInputTokens": 8644, "cacheCreationInputTokens": 4114}, "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001": {"costUSD": 0.0016380000000000001, "provider": "firstParty", "inputTokens": 1543, "outputTokens": 19, "contextWindow": 200000, "canonicalModel": "claude-haiku-4-5", "maxOutputTokens": 32000, "webSearchRequests": 0, "cacheReadInputTokens": 0, "cacheCreationInputTokens": 0}}}
8cd55121-ef45-491e-abf1-4f64741a9caf	65e03a66-6d80-4c3e-99f0-0e3b754cf673	Four accounts made 36 small payments on a single day (28 Aug), all worth 881.85 INR together — amounts from 1 to 50 INR, median about 24 INR. All four used the same two payment instruments, each appearing 17 times, and all four shared one device across 9 transactions, with three of them sharing a second device across 11. That is very heavy sharing: every account touches every instrument. Against that, the behaviour looks manual, not automated — gaps between payments are irregular and average around 4.5 minutes, not machine-fast. The accounts are also established, not throwaways: each has more than three transactions. The money involved is tiny, which cuts both ways: it fits a card-testing probe, but equally fits a family or a small shop's shared tablet and shared cards.	Moderately unsure — the attribute sharing is genuinely unusual and drives almost the whole score, but the tiny amounts, single-day burst, and human-looking timing fit several ordinary explanations just as well.	review_closer	["all 4 accounts used the same two payment instruments, 17 transactions each", "all 4 accounts used one shared device (9 transactions); 3 shared a second (11)", "36 transactions compressed into a single day, 28 Aug 2026", "very small amounts: 1-50 INR, median 24 INR, 881.85 INR total", "timing is irregular and unhurried (median 4.5 min apart) - looks manually driven, not scripted"]	["A household, hostel, or small shop where several people share one or two phones and one or two cards would produce exactly this footprint - and the human-like timing supports that reading.", "Card-testing usually shows failed authorisations and rapid scripted bursts; the evidence given contains no approval/decline outcomes, so this cannot be checked here.", "What would settle it: whether the instruments belong to a cardholder named on any of these accounts, whether the accounts share a registered address or contact details, whe